  <page>
    <title>Arturo Moreno</title>
    <id>229691</id>
    <revision>
      <id>41830678</id>
      <timestamp>2006-03-02T00:49:16Z</timestamp>
      <contributor>
        <username>Gujuguy</username>
        <id>699471</id>
      </contributor>
      <text xml:space="preserve">'''Arturo &quot;Arte&quot; Moreno''' (born [[1946]]) is a [[Mexican American]] billionaire who, on [[May 15]], [[2003]], made history by becoming the first Hispanic to own a major sports team in the [[United States]].

Moreno is a native of [[Tucson, Arizona|Tucson]]. From [[1966]] to [[1968]], he served with the [[Army]], fighting in the [[Vietnam War]].

In [[1973]], he graduated from the [[University of Arizona]], with a degree in marketing. That same year, he was hired to work at a company named [[Karl Eller|Eller Outdoor]]. 

He would travel across the country for the next seven years, Eller Outdoor moving him around several times. However, in [[1984]], he moved back to [[Arizona]], settling in [[Phoenix, Arizona|Phoenix]]. He was hired by [[Outdoor Systems]] there. It was while at ''Outdoor Systems'' that he was first able to make his mark as a businessman: Under his leadership, the company's total profits grew from 500,000 [[US dollars|dollars]] to 90 million dollars in less than ten years. Being that [[baseball]] is his favorite past-time, he bought the minor league team [[Salt Lake Trappers]] alongside 17 other investors, and married his girlfriend Carole in [[1986]].

In [[1996]], Outdoor Systems went public, and in [[1997]], he set up the Moreno Family Foundation. In [[1998]], he sold Outdoor Systems for 8 billion dollars.

By [[2001]], Moreno was hungry to own a [[MLB|Major League Baseball]] team. He tried to buy a controlling part of his hometown's [[Arizona Diamondbacks]], however, no deal could be reached. He insisted on trying to buy a major league team and set his sights on the [[2002 World Series]] champions [[Anaheim Angels]]. On [[April 2003]], it was announced he had reached a deal with [[The Walt Disney Company]], to obtain the team for 180 million dollars. And, in a historic meeting on May 15 of that year, MLB commissioner [[Bud Selig]] announced the sale of the Angels to Moreno had been approved.

One of the first people to congratulate Moreno after the news was Diamondbacks' owner [[Jerry Colangelo]], a personal friend. Colangelo declared that it was a good opportunity for Moreno.

[[Category:1946 births|Moreno, Arturo]]
[[Category:Living people|Moreno, Arturo]]
[[Category:Baseball executives|Moreno, Arturo]]
[[Category:Advertising magnates|Moreno, Arturo]]
[[Category:University of Arizona|Moreno, Arturo]]
[[Category:Mexican American businesspeople|Moreno, Arturo]]
[[Category:Mexican American sportspeople|Moreno, Arturo]]
[[Category:Vietnam War veterans|Moreno, Arturo]]
[[Category:Anaheim Angels|Moreno, Arturo]]
[[Category:Tucsonans|Moreno, Arturo]]</text>
    </revision>
