  <page>
    <title>Walmex</title>
    <id>229705</id>
    <revision>
      <id>42113863</id>
      <timestamp>2006-03-03T22:35:21Z</timestamp>
      <contributor>
        <username>Rockero420</username>
        <id>328895</id>
      </contributor>
      <minor />
      <comment>/* WalMex in Teotihuacán */ link INAH, bypass redirect</comment>
      <text xml:space="preserve">'''Walmex''', or '''Wal-Mart de [[Mexico]]''', is a [[Latin America]]n [[blue-chip]] company, a majority of which is owned by the [[US|U.S.]] giant [[Wal-Mart]] Stores, Inc.

Walmex operates nearly 600 [[retail]] outlets in Mexico including [[restaurant]]s and [[supermarket]]s.

==WalMex in Teotihuacán==

In 2004, Walmex faced opposition by local and environmental groups opposed to the installation of a WalMex-owned [[Bodega Aurrera]] store located near the archeological site of [[Teotihuacán]]. In spite of negative sentiments 
by these groups and the media, the [[National Institute of Anthropology and History]] (INAH) dismissed concerns that construction of the store would destory archeological treasures. The [[Federal government of the United Mexican States|federal government]] also endorsed the project which is located a mile away from the pyramids. The store opened its doors at the end of the year.

==External links==
* [http://memphis.bizjournals.com/memphis/stories/2002/10/14/daily21.html ''Wal-Mart quits Mexican trade group''], article at the [[Memphis Business Journal]].
* [http://retanet.unm.edu/LADB-articles/25788.html Wal-Mart subsidiary receives final approval to construct controversial store near historic Teotihuacán pyramids], article at The [[University of New Mexico]].

&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;
{{Wal-Mart}}

[[Category:Wal-Mart]]
[[Category:Companies listed on the Bolsa Mexicana de Valores]]
[[Category:Supermarkets of Mexico]]
{{company-stub}}
{{mexico-company-stub}}</text>
