  <page>
    <title>Horizontal</title>
    <id>229725</id>
    <revision>
      <id>41127737</id>
      <timestamp>2006-02-25T05:39:48Z</timestamp>
      <contributor>
        <username>Everyking</username>
        <id>44020</id>
      </contributor>
      <minor />
      <comment>Reverted edits by [[Special:Contributions/202.7.166.171|202.7.166.171]] ([[User talk:202.7.166.171|talk]]) to last version by Cacycle</comment>
      <text xml:space="preserve">'''Horizontal''' is an orientation relating to, or in parallel with the [[horizon]], and thus perpendicular to the [[vertical]]. A horizontal line goes from left to right (or vice versa) where a vertical line goes from top to bottom.

In [[Cartesian coordinate system|Cartesian coordinates]] of the form (''x'',''y'') where it is frequently called ''abscissa'', the horizontal axis is conventionally used for the ''x'' coordinate, with [[positive number]]s extending to the right side of the vertical axis.  The equation ''y''=''c'', where ''c'' a constant, represents a horizontal line. In an [[complex number]], the horizontal axis is used for the real component.

In perspective drawing, a set of [[parallel]] horizontal lines extending from the same vicinity must ultimately meet at a single point on the horizon known as the [[vanishing point]].  This is a common feature of pictures of straight roads or railway lines.

In construction, a [[spirit level]] helps orient  structural members absolutely horizontal.

== See also ==

* [[Horizontal coordinate system]]

[[Category:Orientation]]

