    <title>Ilyushin Il-62</title>
    <id>229856</id>
    <revision>
      <id>39507771</id>
      <timestamp>2006-02-14T00:04:01Z</timestamp>
      <contributor>
        <username>ProhibitOnions</username>
        <id>139004</id>
      </contributor>
      <comment>Rm redundancy (possibly mine)</comment>
      <text xml:space="preserve">__NOTOC__
[[Image:Air Koryo IL-62M P-881.JPG|thumb|right|250px|Air Koryo's Il-62M jetliner in [[Beijing Capital International Airport]].]]The '''[[Ilyushin]] Il-62''' is a long range [[Soviet Union|Soviet]] civil [[airliner]], which first flew in [[1963]] and entered service with [[Aeroflot]] in [[1967]]. The first passenger service was an Aeroflot flight from [[Moscow]] to [[Montreal]] on [[15 September]] [[1967]]. It has the [[NATO reporting name]] &quot;Classic&quot;.

It was developed at the same time as the [[United Kingdom|British]] [[Vickers VC-10]], to which it bears a very close resemblance &amp;mdash; [[industrial espionage]] was widely suspected at the time, but has never been proved.

It is powered by four rear-mounted [[Kuznetsov (aircraft engines)|Kuznetsov]] [[Kuznetsov NK-8|NK-8-4]] engines in pairs in a [[T-tail]] arrangement. The VC-10 and Il-62 are the only airliners to have used this configuration.

The Il-62 is one of the largest aircraft without powered flight controls: all moving surfaces are controlled by direct cable links.

There is an extra wheel below the tail to prevent the plane tilting backwards during loading.

The aircraft was designed to withstand gear-up landings, having a strengthened belly with a ski running down the middle.

The '''Il-62M''' variant (first flight in [[1971]], introduced in [[1973]]) has more powerful and efficient [[D-30KU]] engines, and an extra fuel tank in the tail. Nearly all of the planes still in service today are Il-62M models. In [[1978]] the next variant was produced: the '''Il-62MK''' with 195 seats, which is capabable of lifting 2,000 kg (4,400 lb) more than the Il-62M.

Il-62 can still be found in commercial service, mostly with countries of the former Soviet Union but also with [[Cuba]], [[Libya]], and [[North Korea]]. It also sees service with military and governmental operators and as private business jet.

== Specifications (Il-62M) ==
=== General characteristics ===
* '''Crew:''' 5
* '''Capacity:''' 168-174
* '''Length:''' 53.12 m (174 ft 3 in)
* '''Wingspan:''' 43.20 m (141 ft 9 in)
* '''Height:''' 12.35 m (40 ft 6 in)
* '''Wing area:''' 279.5 m² (3,009 ft²)
* '''Empty:''' 67,500 kg (149,000 lb)
* '''Loaded:''' 97,500 kg (215,000 lb)
* '''Maximum takeoff:''' 165,000 kg (364,000 lb)
* '''Powerplant:''' 4× D-30KU; 11,000 [[kgf]] (108 kN (24,300 [[lbf]]) thrust

=== Performance ===
* '''Maximum speed:''' 900 km/h (490 [[knot (speed)|kt]]; 560 [[mile per hour|mph]])
* '''Range:'''  km ( ~5500[[nautical mile|nm]];  [[statute mile|mi]])
* '''Service ceiling:''' 12,000 m (39,000 ft)
* '''Rate of climb:''' 320 m/min (1,000 ft/min)
* '''Wing loading:''' 349 kg/m² (71.4 lb/ft²)
* '''Thrust/weight ratio:''' 0.451:1 (4.43 N/kg; 0.451 lbf/lb)

===Civil Operators (past and present)=== 
* Aeroflot, Air Koryo, Air Ukraine, Alim Airlines, Alpha Airlines, Aviaenergo, CAAC, Centrafricain, CSA, Cubana, Dalavia, Domodedovo, East Line, Egyptair, Interavia, Interflug, Jetline, KAPO, Kokshetau Airlines, Krasair, Libyan Arab Airlines, Lot Polish Airlines, Malev, Mavial Magadan, Mekong Air International, Moscow Airways, New Millennium Air, Quadrotour-Aero, Rossija, Russian Sky, SAT Sakhalin, TAAG Angolan, Tarom, Tretyakovo, Vim, Uzbekistan Airlines, Yana Airlines

===Military Operators (past and present)===
* Gambia, Germany, Georgia, Libya, North Korea, Russia

== External links ==
*[http://www.airliners.net/search/photo.search?aircraft_genericsearch=Ilyushin%20Il-62&amp;distinct_entry=true Pictures of Il-62]

