  <page>
    <title>The Marble Index</title>
    <id>229857</id>
    <revision>
      <id>32013582</id>
      <timestamp>2005-12-19T21:34:09Z</timestamp>
      <contributor>
        <username>Fritz Saalfeld</username>
        <id>128909</id>
      </contributor>
      <comment>[[WP:AWB|Assisted]] clean up: Changed length format in infobox to mm:ss (See [[WP:ALBUMS]])</comment>
      <text xml:space="preserve">{{Album infobox |
  Name        = The Marble Index |
  Type        = [[Vinyl record | LP]] |
  Artist      = [[Nico]] |
  Cover       = NICOMARBLEINDEX.JPG |
  Background  = Orange |
  Released    = [[1969]] |
  Recorded    = [[1968]] |
  Genre       = [[Rock and roll | Rock]] |
  Length      = 30:48 |
  Label       = [[Elektra Records | Elektra]] |
  Producer    = [[Frazier Mohawk]] |
  Reviews     = &lt;nowiki&gt;&lt;/nowiki&gt;
*&lt;li&gt;[[All Music Guide|AMG]] (3/5) [http://www.allmusic.com/cg/amg.dll?p=amg&amp;sql=10:diazefekhgf6 link]&lt;/li&gt;
|
  Last album  = ''[[Chelsea Girl]]'' &lt;br /&gt;([[1968]])|
  This album  = '''''The Marble Index'''''&lt;br /&gt;([[1969]]) |
  Next album  = ''[[Desertshore]]'' &lt;br /&gt; ([[1970]])|
}}
''Where the statue stood''&lt;br/&gt;
''Of Newton, with his prism and silent face,''&lt;br/&gt;
''The '''marble index''' of a mind forever''&lt;br/&gt;
''Voyaging through strange seas of thought alone'' -- '''[[William Wordsworth | W. Wordsworth]]'''&lt;br/&gt;
&lt;br/&gt;
'''''The Marble Index''''' is the name of [[Nico]]'s [[1969]] [[neoclassicism|neoclassical]] and [[avant-garde]] work. 

The album featured long-term associate [[John Cale]], an [[experimental music]]ian who had worked briefly with Nico during her stint in [[The Velvet Underground]]. Cale had an extensive background in various avant-garde settings, working with  [[Minimalism|minimalist]] composer [[LaMonte Young]], among others. Cale and Nico created an album that radically deviated  from traditional rock music song structures. Cale said  it was the first rock album to do so. He also said that '''The Marble Index''' had  made a  seminal contribution to the body of  modern classical music. 

Nico wrote all her own songs on this album and accompanied herself on the [[harmonium]], which has also been referred to as an [[Indian Pump Organ]]. The arrangements are abstract and, musically speaking, &quot;cold&quot;. The effect is kindred to the [[psychology|psychological]] sound experimentation of [[Electronic music|electronic]] and [[serial]] [[composer]], [[Karlheinz Stockhausen]].  The Marble Index has been described as a &quot;nightmare in sound&quot;. It has influenced a wide array of genres in contemporary [[independent record label|independent music]]. Artists such as [[Coil]], [[Jocelyn Pook]] and  [[Dead Can Dance]], as well as numerous contemporary [[Goth music|goth]] bands  have all cited Nico as a seminal influence. 

==Lyrics==

Nico employed images of power, cold, fear, dreams, terror, resignation, and loss (among others) on '''The Marble Index'''.  Her lyrics might be described as [[Romantic]] or [[gothic art|Gothic]] as they  focus primarily on the fragility of life and the imminence of death.

==Track listing==
===Side one===
#&quot;Prelude&quot; - 1:00
#&quot;Lawns of Dawns&quot; - 3:11
#&quot;No One Is There&quot; - 3:37
#&quot;Ari's Song&quot; - 3:21
#&quot;Facing the Wind&quot; - 4:55
===Side two===
#&quot;Julius Caesar (Memento Hodie)&quot; - 5:02
#&quot;Frozen Warnings&quot; - 4:02
#&quot;Evening of Light&quot; - 5:40

CD bonus tracks (previously unreleased):
*&quot;Roses in the Snow&quot; - 4:10 
*&quot;Nibelungen&quot; - 2:43

==Personnel==

;Nico: vocals, [[harmonium]]
;[[John Cale]]: [[electric viola]], piano, bass, electric guitar, [[glockenspiel]], bells, mouth organ, [[bosun's pipe]]

==External links==

*[http://www.gadflyonline.com/06-24-02/music-nico.html Gadfly essay on Nico]
*[http://smironne.free.fr/NICO/marb.php Lyrics, images, liner notes]

{{Footer_Albums_Nico}}

