      <text xml:space="preserve">__NOTOC__
Two aircraft have shared the designation '''Ilyushin Il-18'''.

The first was a propeller-driven airliner of [[1946]] based on the [[B-29 Superfortress]]. After a year of test flights, the programme was abandoned.
----
[[Image:IL-18-700px.jpg|thumb|300px|Il-18 on display at a [[Moscow]]'s [[Domodedovo International Airport]]]]

The second '''Il-18''' is a large [[turboprop]] [[airliner]] resembling the [[Lockheed]] [[L-188 Electra]].

As one of the best known [[Russia]]n aircraft, it is also one of the most popular and long lasting, first flying in [[1965]].

Seating 100 to 120 passengers, its operating economics are such that many are still in service in [[Cuba]], [[Siberia]] and by some operators in the [[Middle East]].

Military versions have also been developed, like the '''Il-20 Coot-A''' [[ELINT]], radar reconnaissance version, and the '''Il-22 Coot-B''' airborne command post aircraft, plus the  [[Ilyushin Il-24|Il-24]] [[ELINT]] version. The [[Ilyushin Il-38|Il-38]] is the maritime reconnaissance, [[anti-submarine warfare]] version.

''This [[aircraft]] article is missing some or all of its [[Wikipedia:WikiProject Aircraft/page content|specifications]]. If you have a source, you can [[Wikipedia:Find or fix a stub|help Wikipedia]] by [{{SERVER}}{{localurl:{{NAMESPACE}}:{{PAGENAME}}|action=edit}} adding them]''.[[Category:Aircraft without specifications]]
