  <page>
    <title>Folland</title>
    <id>229863</id>
    <revision>
      <id>40334512</id>
      <timestamp>2006-02-19T22:06:04Z</timestamp>
      <contributor>
        <username>TPO-bot</username>
        <id>725128</id>
      </contributor>
      <minor />
      <comment>Robot: Changing template: aero-corp-stub</comment>
      <text xml:space="preserve">[[Image:Gnat.750pix.jpg|300px|thumb|right|Privately owned Folland Gnat]]

'''Folland''' was the builder of the [[Folland Gnat]] lightweight fighter and trainer, designed by [[W. E. W. Petter]] who had previously designed the [[Westland Lysander|Lysander]], [[English Electric Canberra|Canberra]] and [[English Electric Lightning|Lightning]].

This aircraft pictured was previously used by the [[Royal Air Force|RAF]] [[Red Arrows]] airshow team, but is shown here painted as an aircraft of the earlier ''Yellowjacks'' display team, a forerunner of the Red Arrows.

==History==
British Marine Aircraft Ltd. was formed in February 1936 to produce [[Sikorsky S-42|Sikorsky S-42-A]] [[flying boat]]s under licence in the UK. The company built a factory on the western side of the [[Hamble]] peninsular with a slipway to [[Southampton Water]]. The Sikorsky deal came to naught, however, and the company was sold to [[Henry Folland|Henry P. Folland]] &lt;!--some sources style him Harold P Folland, probably a back-formation from Harry; there is no Harold or Harry Folland in the 1901 census--&gt;, formerly Chief designer for [[Gloster Aircraft Company]], who renamed it Folland Aircraft Limited on [[December 24]] [[1937]]. {{inote|Fagan|Fagan}} Folland began aircraft assembly at Hamble making parts for [[Bristol Blenheim]] and [[Beaufort]] bombers. Folland later took on sub-contract work making parts for [[de Havilland Mosquito]]s and [[Vickers Wellington]]s.{{inote|Haselden|Haselden}}

The company flew the first aircraft of its own design, the [[Folland 43/47]] (Design number FO 108) in [[1940]].

[[W. E. W. Petter]] joined the company as [[managing director]] in [[1950]]. {{inote|British Aircraft Directory|BAD}} He designed the [[Folland Midge]], which first flew [[August 11]] [[1954]]{{inote|British Aircraft Directory|BAD}}, and the [[Folland Gnat]] training jet ([[July 18]] [[1955]]){{inote|British Aircraft Directory|BAD}}. Both were built at Hamble until the [[1960s]]. In [[1959]] Folland was acquired by [[Hawker Siddeley]], and ultimately became part of [[British Aerospace]] (BAe). Although Hamble airfield closed in [[1986]], BAe continued to use the premises there for the production of major assemblies for [[Harrier]] and [[Hawk]] jets. {{inote|Haselden|Haselden}}

==References==
*[http://www.britishaircraft.co.uk/companypage.php?ID=21 'Folland', ''British Aircraft Directory''] ([[May 1]] [[2004]]) Retrieved [[May 20]] [[2005]]
*[http://daveg4otu.tripod.com/airfields/ham.html Fagan, Dave. 'Hamble' ''Aviation in Hampshire UK 1900 to 2000''] Retrieved [[May 20]] [[2005]]
*[http://www.delscope.demon.co.uk/personal/hamble.htm Haselden, Derek. ''Aviation at Hamble''] ([[January 8]] [[2005]]) Retrieved [[May 20]] [[2005]]
{{airlistbox}}

[[Category:Defunct British aircraft manufacturers]]

{{aero-company-stub}}</text>
    </revision>
