The [[North American Aviation|North American]] '''F-100 Super Sabre''' was a [[jet engine|jet]] [[fighter aircraft]] that served with the [[United States Air Force|USAF]] from [[1954 in aviation|1954]] to [[1971 in aviation|1971]] and with the [[Air National Guard|ANG]] until [[1979 in aviation|1979]]. It was the successor to the [[F-86 Sabre]], the first of the [[century series]] of U.S. jet fighters, and the first U.S. fighter capable of [[supersonic]] speed in level flight. In its later life it was often referred to as &quot;the Hun&quot;, a shortened version of &quot;one hundred.&quot;

Development work began in 1949 for a supersonic interceptor, and prototype construction started in [[1951 in aviation|1951]], when the company won a contract for 110 aircraft. The first '''YF-100''' prototype flew on [[May 25]], [[1953]] with a [[Pratt &amp; Whitney J57]] engine, it broke the sound-barrier on its first flight. The '''YF-100''' set a world speed record in October [[1953 in aviation|1953]] at 754.99 mph (1,215.04 km/h). The first aircraft, the '''F-100A''', were delivered in late 1953, and became operational from September 1954. Despite the testing, there were a number of operational crashes that grounded the aircraft from November 1954 until February 1955 when the problem (inertia-coupling) was identified. 

Put briefly, under conditions of high speed and high [[g-load|''g''-load]], the aircraft could develop a sudden roll and yaw which would happen too fast for the pilot to correct and would quickly stress the aircraft structure to disintegration. It was under these conditions that North American's chief test pilot, [[George Welch]], was killed while dive testing an early-production F-100A on [[October 12]], [[1954]]. At lower speeds and loading this can be controlled but military combat aircraft have to be able to vigorously manuever to the edges of the 'flight envelope' so this had to be rectified. The solution was found by extending the wing span by 26 inches (66 cm) and increasing the area of the vertical tail by 27%.

A related control problem that stemmed from handling characteristics associated with the swept-back wing as the aircraft approached stall speed led to a situation known as the [[Sabre dance]] in which the pilot was unable to get the plane back into the flight envelope despite applying full power, usually with disastrous results. 

The '''F-100A''' was followed by the '''F-100B''' (which was re-designated [[F-107|F-107A]]), the '''F-100C''' (1954, 476 built) and the '''F-100D''' (1956, 1,274 built) fighter-bombers.

The &quot;D-model&quot; gave up any pretense of being an air superiority fighter and was equipped with increased wing area, greater fin and rudder size, six underwing hardpoints, the addition of flaps to help lower landing speed, and improved electronics. Among the electronic improvements was an autopilot and the addition of a LABS (Low Altitude Bombing System) whose principal purpose was to enable the F-100 to &quot;lob&quot; nuclear weapons (typically a single weapon carried under the the belly of the plane) at a target to enhance the chances of survival for the airplane and pilot. The final 444 examples built were equipped to fire the &quot;B-model&quot; of the [[Sidewinder]] [[AIM-9]] [[air-to-air missile]], and all USAF &quot;D's&quot; were eventually modified to fire the [[AGM-12 Bullpup]] [[air-to-surface missile]] which was steered by the pilot using radio control.

The '''F-100D''' was an unforgiving aircraft, with over 500 lost in accidents by USAF pilots. The final production variant was the '''F-100F''' tandem [[trainer]] (339 built), first flown in [[1956 in aviation|1956]]. It was stretched by 3 m to accommodate the second crew and the cannon armament was reduced from four to two.

The Super Sabre, especially the '''F-100D''' was widely used in the [[Vietnam War]] as a ground attack aircraft mostly within South Vietnam. The two-seat F-100F also saw combat action in Vietnam. It was operated both as a &quot;fast-FAC&quot; ([[forward air controller]]) spotting targets for other aircraft and was the aircraft used by the first &quot;[[Wild Weasel]]&quot; unit, whose specialized crews went hunting for enemy radars and anti-aircraft sites to shut down or destroy in order to protect the rest of the strike force.

The F-100 was progressively replaced in Vietnam action from 1966 by the [[F-4 Phantom II|F-4]] and the [[F-105 Thunderchief|F-105]]. The aircraft served in Vietnam until 1971 and even later into the 1970s in USAF [[Air National Guard]] and Air Force Reserve units until replaced by [[A-7]] Corsair II's, [[F-4]]'s, and [[A-10]]'s.

The &quot;Hun&quot; also served as the mount of the USAF &quot;Thunderbirds&quot; aerial demonstration team from 1956 and the similar &quot;Skyblazers&quot; of the USAF Europe operated the aircraft from 1958. The Super Sabre also served with the air forces of several countries other than the United States. France, Turkey, Denmark, and Taiwan all operating the F-100 for many years.

The F-100 was the subject of many modification programs over the course of its service. Many of these were improvements to electronics, structural strengthening, and projects to improve maintainability. One of the more interesting of these was the replacement of the original afterburner of the J-57 engine, a simple &quot;on-off&quot; device that was long a maintenance headache, with the more advanced afterburners from retired Convair [[F-102]] Delta Dagger interceptors. The [[F-102]] also used a model of the J-57 for power, and the interceptor's afterburner was more reliable and offered some ability to modulate the power it produced. This modification also changed the appearance of the aft end of the F-100, doing away with the original &quot;petal-style&quot; exhaust.

After the Super Sabres were withdrawn from service a large number of them were converted into remote-controlled drones for use as targets for various antiaircraft weapons.

USAF [[Colonel]] and [[Medal of Honor]] recipent [[George E. Day|George E. &quot;Bud&quot; Day]] flew F-100s.

===Operators===
* Denmark, France, Taiwan, Turkey, United States (Air Force),

