  <page>
    <title>Green!</title>
    <id>229958</id>
    <revision>
      <id>40169624</id>
      <timestamp>2006-02-18T18:22:46Z</timestamp>
      <contributor>
        <username>Eskimbot</username>
        <id>477460</id>
      </contributor>
      <minor />
      <comment>robot  Adding: eo</comment>
      <text xml:space="preserve">{{Politics of Belgium}}
{{Greens}}
'''Groen!''' (English ''Green!'') is the [[Flanders|Flemish]] [[Green party|green]] [[political party|party]] in [[Belgium]]. 

Founded in [[1981]] as '''Agalev''', the party has maintained good relations with its Francophone sister party, [[Ecolo]]. The name &quot;Agalev&quot; is short for &quot;''Anders GAan LEVen''&quot; (&quot;going to live differently&quot;). It was the result of a grassroots progressive environmental movement initiated by progressive Catholics, alternative socialists and environmentalists. The party was part of [[Guy Verhofstadt]]'s first coalition ([[1999]]-[[2003]]), but lost all its seats in the federal parliament mainly to [[Socialistische Partij - Anders|SP.a]]-[[Spirit (political party)|Spirit]] cartel in the May 18, [[2003]], elections.

In June 2003, the party gave permission to local divisions to form cartels with other parties, more specifically SP.A, which had already formed a cartel with the social [[liberal]] Spirit. On November 14, [[2003]], [[Vera Dua]] replaced [[Dirk Holemans]] as the party president, a post which Agalev until then called &quot;political secretary&quot;. By then Agalev was fighting for its life as an independent Flemish progressive green party.

During a party congress, Agalev's name was changed to '''Groen!''' Though Agalev-[[Limburg (Belgium)|Limburg]] voted in favor of a cartel with the [[socialist]] SP.A party, the party congress rejected this option. Groen! decided to go to the regional and European elections as an independent party. Groen! held its ground in both elections by regaining votes from the [[Socialistische Partij - Anders|SP.a]]-[[Spirit (political party)|Spirit]] cartel and gained seats in the Flemish parliament. Citing its still electoral risky position as the main reason, Groen! ruled out any participation in a new Flemish government. 

==European Parliament==
The party has one member in the European Parliament, [[Bart Staes]].

==See also==
*[[Ecolo]], the French-speaking Green party in Belgium
*[[Jong Groen!]], the Groen! youth section

==External links==
*[http://www.groen.be Groen!] (Green!)
*[http://www.jonggroen.be Jong Groen!] (Young Green!)
*[http://jade.studentenweb.org Jade] ([[Leuven]] student division of Jong Groen!)
*[http://student.ugent.be/gras Gras] ([[Ghent]] student division of Jong Groen!)
*[http://www.vrtnieuws.net/nieuwsnet_master/default/nieuws/dossiers/PARTIJ_agalev_overzicht/index.html Dossier Groen! - collection of news articles and video fragments at VRTnieuws.net (in Dutch)]
*[http://www.standaard.be/extra/verkiezingen2004/index.asp?DosID=2472 Dossier Groen! - collection of newspaper articles and their results in the 2004 elections at De Standaard (in Dutch)]

