  <page>
    <title>Wolf-Rayet star</title>
    <id>229988</id>
    <revision>
      <id>41705793</id>
      <timestamp>2006-03-01T04:43:54Z</timestamp>
      <contributor>
        <ip>216.85.68.66</ip>
      </contributor>
      <text xml:space="preserve">[[Image:Wolf-rayet.jpg|thumb|right|Artist's impression of a Wolf-Rayet star]]

'''Wolf-Rayet stars''' (often referred to as WR stars) are evolved, hot, massive [[star]]s which have very strong [[solar wind|stellar winds]]. Wolf-Rayet stars are a normal stage in the evolution of massive stars, in which strong, broad [[spectral line|emission lines]] of helium and nitrogen (&quot;WN&quot; sequence) or helium, carbon, and nitrogen (&quot;WC&quot; sequence) are visible.

Due to their strong emission lines they are relatively easy to identify in nearby galaxies. About 150 Wolf-Rayets are known in our own [[Milky Way Galaxy]], about 100 are known in the [[Large Magellanic Cloud]], while only 12 have been identified in the [[Small Magellanic Cloud]].  Wolf-Rayet stars were discovered [[spectrometer|spectroscopically]] in 1867 by the French astronomers [[Charles Wolf]] and [[Georges Rayet]] using visual spectrometery at [[Paris Observatory]].

A recent campaign to image several Wolf-Rayet binaries at high resolution has found specimens which are surrounded by [[pinwheel nebula|pinwheel nebulae]], with dust created by the interaction of winds in a close binary system.

Furthermore some (roughly 10% of the galactic) central stars of [[planetary nebula|planetary nebulae]] are - despite of their lower masses - also of the WR-type, i.e. they show emission line spectra with broad lines from helium, carbon and oxygen.

The most known (as well as visible) example of Wolf-Rayet star is [[Gamma Velorum|Gamma Velorum]] (γ Vel), which is a bright star visible to those located south of 40 degrees northern latitude. One of the members of the star system (gamma Velorum is actually four stars) is a Wolf-Rayet star. Due to the exotic nature of it's spectrum (bright emission lines in lieu of dark absorption lines) it is dubbed as &quot;The spectral Gem of the southern sky&quot;.

[[Image:Wolf rayet2.jpg|thumb|right|Hubble Space Telescope image of nebula M1-67 around Wolf Rayet star WR 124]]

==See also==
*[[Wolf-Rayet galaxy]]
*[[Wolf-Rayet nebula]]

==External links==
*[http://www.physics.usyd.edu.au/~gekko/wr104.html] Some Wolf-Rayet stars in binaries are close enough that we can image a rotating &quot;[[pinwheel nebula]]&quot; showing the dust generated by colliding winds in the binary system, from [[Aperture Masking Interferometry]] observations.
*[http://cfa-www.harvard.edu/~pberlind/atlas/htmls/wrstars.html]
*[http://www.astro.lsa.umich.edu/~monnier/Publications/monnier1999c.pdf] (PDF)
*[http://uk.arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0501391]

[[Category:Star types]]
