  <page>
    <title>Northern Ireland Railways</title>
    <id>230014</id>
    <revision>
      <id>40313857</id>
      <timestamp>2006-02-19T19:25:12Z</timestamp>
      <contributor>
        <username>MartinRe</username>
        <id>696743</id>
      </contributor>
      <minor />
      <comment>Disambiguate [[Miles]] to [[Mile]] using [[:en:Wikipedia:Tools/Navigation_popups|popups]]</comment>
      <text xml:space="preserve">[[Image:Translink northernirelandrailways.PNG|thumb|right|Current NIR logo]]

'''Northern Ireland Railways''' ('''NIR''' or '''NI Railways'''), formerly and briefly referred to as '''Ulster Transport Railways''' (''UTR'') is the [[railway]] operator responsible for running the railway network in [[Northern Ireland]]. Currently the sole-surviving state-owned train operator in the UK, it is a subsidiary of the [[Northern Ireland Transport Holdings Company|Northern Ireland Transport Holding Company]], and has a common Board of Management with the other two transport Companies, [[Ulsterbus]] and [[Citybus (Belfast)|Citybus]], trading under the [[brand name]] [[Translink (Northern Ireland)|Translink]]. 

==Routes==
NI Railways currently operate two important lines between [[Belfast]] and [[Derry]] and between Belfast and the border with the [[Republic of Ireland]] then to [[Dundalk]]. On the latter route NIR operates a joint service of fast trains (the ''[[Enterprise (train)|Enterprise]]'') between [[Belfast]] and [[Dublin]] in conjunction with the national railway company of the Republic of Ireland, [[Iarnród Éireann]]. NIR also operates a branch line between [[Coleraine]] and [[Portrush]], and suburban services from Belfast to [[Bangor, Northern Ireland|Bangor]], [[Larne]], and [[Portadown]].
For more details visit:
*[[Dublin-Belfast railway line]]
*[[Belfast-Bangor railway line]]
*[[Belfast-Larne railway line]]
*[[Belfast-Londonderry railway line]]
*[[Coleraine-Portrush railway line]] 
*[[Belfast-Newry railway line]] 

==History==
[[Image:Northern Ireland Railways.png|thumb|right|180px|A former logo of the company]]
NIR was formed in [[1968]] when it took over from the [[Ulster Transport Authority]], which operated since [[1948]]. Much of Northern Ireland's railway network was closed during the [[1950s]] and [[1960s]], declining from 1500 [[Kilometre|km]] (900 [[Mile|miles]]) in the 1950s to 342 km (210 miles) at present. 

Since the 1960s the NIR network became increasingly run down, with old rolling stock and worn out track. In [[2004]]/[[2005]], NI Railways began to receive delivery of 23 new trainsets from [[Construcciones y Auxiliar de Ferrocarriles|CAF]] of Spain, in an order which totalled £80m. The final train, 3023, arrived in Belfast Harbour on 18 July 2005. All had entered service by 24.09.05, with each one having to complete 2000 miles of fault-free running before entering service.

==See also==
*[[Diesel Locomotives of Ireland]]
*[[Rail transport in Ireland]]
*[[History of rail transport in Ireland]]

==External links==
*[http://www.nirailways.co.uk/nir.asp Official site]
*[http://bbs.keyhole.com/ubb/showthreaded.php/Cat/0/Number/249196 Collection of Google Earth locations of NI Railways stations] (Requires [http://earth.google.com Google Earth software]) from the Google Earth Community forum.

