  <page>
    <title>Charles Augustin Sainte-Beuve</title>
    <id>230022</id>
    <revision>
      <id>40566937</id>
      <timestamp>2006-02-21T14:02:44Z</timestamp>
      <contributor>
        <username>Palica</username>
        <id>188933</id>
      </contributor>
      <minor />
      <comment>robot  Adding: sk</comment>
      <text xml:space="preserve">{{French literature (small)}}

'''Charles Augustin Sainte-Beuve''' ([[December 23]], [[1804]] &amp;ndash; [[October 13]], [[1869]]) was a literary critic and one of the major figures of [[France|French]] literary history. He was born in [[Boulogne-sur-Mer|Boulogne]], educated there, and studied medicine at the [[Collège Charlemagne]] in [[Paris]] ([[1824]]&amp;#45;[[1827|27]]).  In [[1828]], he served in the St. Louis Hospital.  Beginning in [[1824]], he contributed literary articles, the ''Premier lundis'' of his collected ''Works'', to the ''Globe'' newspaper, and in [[1827]] he came, through through a review of [[Victor Hugo|Hugo's]] ''Odes et ballads'', into close association with that poet and the [[C&amp;#233;nacle]].  He became friendly with [[Victor Hugo]] after publishing a favourable review of the author's work, and had an affair with Hugo's wife.

One of Sainte-Beuve's major critical contentions was that in order to understand an artist it was first necessary to understand that artist's biography.  [[Marcel Proust]] took issue with this contention and began an essay meant to refute it.  Proust's essay eventually developed into ''[[In Search of Lost Time|À la recherche du temps perdu]]'', ironically a very autobiographical work.

Sainte-Beuve was made a member of the [[Académie française|French Academy]] in [[1845]].  During the turmoil of [[1848]] in [[Europe]], he lectured at [[Li&amp;#232;ge (city)|Li&amp;#233;ge]] on ''Chateaubriand et son groupe litt&amp;#233;raire''.  He returned to [[Paris, France|Paris]] in [[1849]] and began his series of Monday chats (''Causeries du lundi'') in the ''Constitutionnel''.  When [[Louis Napoleon]] became Emperor he made Sainte&amp;#45;Beuve professor of [[Latin (language)|Latin]] poetry at the [[Coll&amp;#232;ge de France]], but anti&amp;#45;Imperialist students hissed him and he resigned.  He was made Senator in [[1865]], in which capacity he distinguished himself by his pleas for freedom of speech and of the press.  In his last years he was an acute sufferer and lived much in retirement. 

Sainte&amp;#45;Beuve had vast knowledge, wonderful tact, and acute perception of what was vital and significant in his subjects.  A selection of the ''Causeries'' in [[English (language)|English]] appeared as ''English Portraits'' (New York, 1875) and another as ''Essays on Men and Women'' (London, 1890).  E. J. Trechmann published a translation (eight volumes, New York, 1909&amp;#45;11).        

==Works==
*''Tableau de la poésie française au seizième siècle'' ([[1828]])
*''Vie, poésies et pensées de Joseph Delorme'' ([[1829]])
*''Les Consolations'' ([[1830]]) (poetry)
*''Volupté'' ([[1835]]) (novel)
*''Port-Royal'' ([[1840]]&amp;ndash;[[1859]])
*''Les Lundis'' ([[1851]]&amp;ndash;[[1872]])
*''Causeries du lundi'' ([[1851]]&amp;ndash;[[1862]])
*''Nouveaux Lundis'' ([[1863]]&amp;ndash;[[1870]])

==English translations==
[http://www.bartleby.com/32/202.html What Is a Classic?]

