  <page>
    <title>Rodeo Rosie</title>
    <id>230138</id>
    <revision>
      <id>32511928</id>
      <timestamp>2005-12-23T19:19:02Z</timestamp>
      <contributor>
        <username>NekoDaemon</username>
        <id>239574</id>
      </contributor>
      <minor />
      <comment>[[Cat#Communication|Nyaa]]! [[Template talk:Categoryredirect|Categoryredirect]]: [[Category:Sesame Street muppet characters]] → [[Category:Sesame Street Muppet characters]]</comment>
      <text xml:space="preserve">'''Rodeo Rosie''' was a character that debuted on [[Sesame Street]] in [[1974]]. A [[Muppet]] dressed in stereotypical clothing of the [[American Old West]], she was often paired with [[Forgetful Jones]], who was similarly dressed. She had a loud voice, yodeled a fair bit, and often bragged.

Far more successful a character was [[Clementine]], the girlfriend of [[Forgetful Jones]].

[[Category:Sesame Street Muppet characters]]</text>
    </revision>
  </page>
  <page>
    <title>List of Sesame Street characters by last known appearance</title>
    <id>230139</id>
    <revision>
      <id>39361702</id>
      <timestamp>2006-02-12T17:36:32Z</timestamp>
      <contributor>
        <username>Deanmo19</username>
        <id>549947</id>
      </contributor>
      <text xml:space="preserve">The following is a list of characters on [[Sesame Street]], ordered by date of last known appearance.

{{inc-video}} 

* [[Grandma Happy]], 1969
* [[Roosevelt Franklin]], 1975, when Matt Robinson left the show, having him voice segments was a lot harder
* [[Sam the Robot]], 1972 (episode #406) to 1976
* [[Captain Vegetable]],  1982
* [[Firefighter Jackson]], 1983 
* [[Beetles]], 1983
* [[Deena and Pearl]], 1983
* [[Mr. Hooper]], 1983. Actor [[Will Lee]] died in late in 1982.
* [[Guy Smiley]], 1990
* [[Flo Bear]], 1991
* [[Sherlock Hemlock]], 1991
* [[Forgetful Jones]] and [[Clementine]], 1992 (both retired when Richard Hunt died)
* [[Gladys the Cow]], 1992 (when Richard Hunt died, character revived in 2003)
* [[Placido Flamingo]], 1992 (when Richard Hunt died)
* [[Sully and Biff]], 1993
* [[Bruno (Sesame Street)|Bruno]], 1993
* [[Little Chrissy and the Alphabeats]], 1993 (with an appearance in 1998)
* [[Buster the Horse]], 1994 (two years after his owner, Forgetful Jones)
* [[Colambo]], 1996
* [[Betty Lou]], 1996
* [[Joey Monkey]] and [[Davey Monkey]], 1997 (although they made appearances in 2004)
* [[Benny Rabbit]], 1998 (although he made appearances in 2001, 2002)
* [[Wolfgang the Seal]], 1999
* [[Elizabeth]], 2002

==Back from retirement==
* [[Grungetta Grunge]], was retired from the show in 1999, making a background appearance in 2004. However, she rejoined the cast in season 36 (2005), as a candidate in &quot;The Grouch Apprentice&quot; skit.

[[Category:Lists of fictional characters|Sesame Street characters by last known appearance]]
[[Category:Sesame Street characters| ]]</text>
    </revision>
  </page>
  <page>
    <title>Fritz Houtermans</title>
    <id>230140</id>
    <revision>
      <id>40692464</id>
      <timestamp>2006-02-22T09:44:12Z</timestamp>
      <contributor>
        <username>Qero</username>
        <id>417430</id>
      </contributor>
      <minor />
      <text xml:space="preserve">'''Friedrich Georg Houtermans''' ([[January 22]], [[1903]] &amp;ndash; [[March 1]], [[1966]]) was a [[physicist]] born in Zoppod (today [[Sopot]], [[Poland]]) near Danzig (today [[Gdansk]], [[Poland]]).

Houtermans made important contributions to [[geochemistry]] and [[cosmochemistry]].

Houtermans married [[Charlotte Riefenstahl]], who had befriended [[Robert Oppenheimer]] when she and he were students in [[Göttingen]]. Charlotte was teaching at [[Vassar]].

== External references ==
*Houtermans, F. (1953) &quot;Determination of the Age of the Earth from the Isotopic Composition of Meteoritic Lead.&quot; Nuovo Cimento 10, 1623-1633,
*Patterson C. (1956) &quot;Age of meteorites and the Earth&quot; Geochimica et Cosmochimica Acta 10, 230-237
*Marvin U.B. (2002) &quot;Oral histories in meteoritics and planetary sciences: VIII Friedrich Begemann&quot;, Meteoritics &amp; Planteary Sciences 37 B69-B77.

{{physicist-stub}}

[[Category:Polish physicists|Houtermans, Fritz]]
[[Category:1903 births|Houtermans, Fritz]]
[[Category:1966 deaths|Houtermans, Fritz]]

[[de:Fritz G. Houtermans]]</text>
    </revision>
  </page>
  <page>
    <title>Karl Daub</title>
    <id>230141</id>
    <revision>
      <id>28134820</id>
      <timestamp>2005-11-12T19:07:54Z</timestamp>
      <contributor>
        <username>Bluebot</username>
        <id>527862</id>
      </contributor>
      <minor />
      <comment>Standardising 1911 references.</comment>
      <text xml:space="preserve">'''Karl Daub''' ([[March 20]], [[1765]] - [[November 22]], [[1836]]), was a [[Germany|German]] [[Protestant]] [[theology|theologian]].

He was born at [[Cassel]].  He studied [[philosophy]], [[philology]] and [[theology]] at [[university of Marburg|Marburg]] in 1786, and eventually (1795) became professor ordinarius of theology at the [[University of Heidelberg]], where he remained until his death.

Daub was one of the leaders of a school which sought to reconcile theology and philosophy, and to bring about a speculative reconstruction of orthodox [[dogma]]. In the course of his intellectual development, he came successively under the influence of [[Immanuel Kant]], [[Friedrich von Schelling]] and [[Georg Wilhelm Friedrich Hegel|Georg Hegel]], and on account of the different phases through which he passed he was called the [[Talleyrand]] of German thought. There was one great defect in his speculative theology: he ignored historical criticism. His purpose was, as [[Otto Pfleiderer]] says,
&lt;blockquote&gt;
&quot;to connect the metaphysical ideas, which had been arrived at by means of philosophical dialectic, directly with the persons and events of the Gospel narratives, thus raising these above the region of ordinary experience into that of the supernatural, and regarding the most absurd assertions as philosophically justified. Daub had become so hopelessly addicted to this perverse principle that he deduced not only Jesus as the embodiment of the philosophical idea of the union of God and man, but also Judas Iscariot as the embodiment of the idea of a rival god, or Satan.&quot;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;

The three stages in Daub's development are clearly marked in his writings. His ''Lehrbuch der Katechetik'' (1801) was written under the spell of Kant. His ''Theologumena'' (1806), his ''Einleitung in das Studium der christi. Dogmatik'' (1810), and his ''Judas Ischarioth'' (2 vols., 1816, 2nd ed., 1818), were all written in the spirit of Schelling, the last of them reflecting a change in Schelling himself from theosophy to positive philosophy. Daub's ''Die dogmatische Theologiejetziger Zeit oder die Selbstsucht in der Wissenschaft des Glaubens'' (1833), and ''Vorlesungen uber die Prolegomena zur Dogmatik'' (1839), are Hegelian in principle and obscure in language. 

==References==
*{{1911}}
[[Category:German theologians|Daub, K]]

[[Category:1765 births|Daub, Karl]]
[[Category:1836 deaths|Daub, Karl]]</text>
    </revision>
  </page>
